common_passwords.txt — offline common/breached-password screening corpus
=========================================================================

Source : SecLists — Passwords/Common-Credentials
         https://github.com/danielmiessler/SecLists
         Two members of the Pwdb series, in this order:
           1. Pwdb_top-10000.txt      all 10,000 entries (the original bundle)
           2. Pwdb_top-1000000.txt    the 5,256 entries that CLEAR the shipped password
                                      policy, were not already covered, and
                                      survive the hygiene filter below
License: MIT (SecLists is MIT-licensed; the underlying lists aggregate publicly
         disclosed breach corpora). Redistributed here under that license.

Contents: 15,256 lines / 15,045 distinct entries (case-insensitively), one per
line, used by messagefoundry.auth.policy as an offline membership check
(ASVS 6.2.4/6.2.12) so a local password that is a known-common/breached value is
rejected. No network call (no live HIBP lookup); a fuller k-anonymity (SHA-1
prefix) corpus is a planned follow-up. The file is data, never executed.

Why a SUBSET of the second file (BACKLOG #1134)
-----------------------------------------------
ASVS 6.2.4 asks for a check against "at least the top 3000 passwords WHICH MATCH
THE APPLICATION'S PASSWORD POLICY". At the shipped `password_min_length = 15`,
only 18 of the original 10,000 entries reached that length and only 4 sat in the
top 3,000 — so the screen added almost nothing on top of the length rule.

An entry the policy would already reject on another clause can only reject what
that clause already rejects. So the second file was filtered through the real
`PasswordPolicy` object rather than a length check, with `check_breached` off
(it would otherwise reject candidates against the very corpus being extended)
and `check_username` off (a corpus entry has no user context). Policy-clearing
coverage is now **5,274**, measured through the shipped loader.

Using the policy object rather than a length test is what makes the result
literally "the top N which match the application's password policy": it applies
the length clause AND the context deny-list. The context clause is why zero of
the 5,274 contain a term from `CONTEXT_WORDS` — they were filtered out at build
time, not left in and tolerated. So the clause reads as contributing nothing
today (removing it changes the count by 0) precisely BECAUSE it already ran.
Adding a term would cut real entries: `qwerty` would remove 98 of the 5,274,
`love` 78, `2010` 30. `password_check_context` therefore belongs beside
`password_min_length` in this corpus's re-score trigger — both settings feed the
filter, and a change to either invalidates the count below.

Policy-clearing entries by floor (`min_length`), measured through the loader:

    floor:    8      10     12     15*    16     17     20
    count:  9,135  6,020  5,367  5,274  3,173  1,735    512
                                  ^ shipped default

The ASVS 6.2.4 bar is 3,000. The shipped floor clears it with one step of
headroom: at 16 the corpus still holds 3,173, and at 17 it falls to 1,735 and
the gate in `tests/test_auth_core.py` goes red. Raising the shipped default past
16 means regenerating this file, not editing the gate.

Integrity
---------
Digest of this corpus, over its bytes exactly as stored and shipped:

    sha256  136e7bcfa519a8881da7e6bf872691a8766ccf11a12db38216b3cef5bc808b80

Reproduce it with any tool, on any platform:

    sha256sum messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.txt

This value is only meaningful because the file is pinned `-text` in
`.gitattributes`, which turns OFF git's line-ending conversion for it. Without
that pin the digest would be true only where `core.autocrlf` happened to match
the recorder's: measured on one commit of this same file, `git hash-object`
returned `4482f231...` under `autocrlf=true` and `7ca7ef4a...` under
`autocrlf=false`, and a wheel built on Windows would have shipped CRLF matching
neither. Do not remove the pin without re-recording this digest.

The UPSTREAM blob digests were not recorded when this corpus was built, and the
revision that added this section did not fetch the upstream to add them, so they
are honestly absent rather than reconstructed. A digest copied from a source
nobody re-fetched is not provenance.

KNOW WHAT THIS DIGEST DOES AND DOES NOT COVER. `tests/test_auth_core.py` checks
it on every CI run, so it catches drift between this file and the corpus in the
SOURCE TREE. It is not an operator-side tamper control: `messagefoundry/
integrity.py` attests installed `.py` files against the wheel's RECORD and skips
data files, so an in-place edit of an INSTALLED corpus is not currently detected.
Closing that is a separate change to the attestation, not to this file.

Hygiene filter
--------------
Three candidate entries were dropped that the policy would
otherwise have accepted, and BOTH classes were found by this repository's commit gates rather
than anticipated: one carrying a C0 control byte (invisible in an editor, in `git diff` and in
review), and two shaped like routable IPv4 addresses. The leak gate's own message says a host
string must be REMOVED rather than allowlisted -- allowlisting would train a reviewer to wave
through the exact shape that gate exists to catch, to keep three worthless corpus entries.

Why the original 10,000 were KEPT rather than replaced
-------------------------------------------------------
At `min_length = 15` they are all redundant — the length clause rejects them
first. But `password_min_length` is an OPERATOR SETTING. A site that lowers it
makes every short entry load-bearing again, so replacing the file would silently
delete their protection for exactly the configuration that needs it most.

Ordering
--------
Both runs are rank-ordered (most common first) within themselves, so the file is
two ordered runs rather than one. This does not affect behaviour: the loader
builds a `frozenset` and only membership is consulted.
